@[expose] public section

noncomputable section
open Classical

namespace ContinuousFOL

/-!
A *countable, recursive* presentation of a metric signature `𝓛` models the
paper's standing assumption for the decidability discussion.

# Paper quotes

§1: "When is the set of all consequences of a theory (in a countable, recursive
language) recursive? […] As in classical first-order logic, it follows from the
completeness theorem of continuous first-order logic that if a complete theory
admits a recursive (or even recursively enumerable) axiomatization then it is
decidable."

Definition 9.10: "Let \(T\) be a theory. (i) If \(T\) is complete, we say \(T\)
is *decidable* if for every sentence \(\varphi\), the value \(\varphi^{\circ}_T\)
is a recursive real and uniformly computable from \(\varphi\). (ii) If \(T\) is
incomplete, we say \(T\) is *decidable* if for every sentence \(\varphi\) the
real number \(\varphi^{\circ}_T\) is uniformly recursive from \(\varphi\)."

Corollary 9.11: "Every complete theory with a recursive or recursively
enumerable axiomatization is decidable."

# Why the earlier fields did not formalize 9.11

The previous signature of `RecursivePresentation` was: an injective numbering
of formulae, with a decidable set of codes; computable \(\dot{-}\); computable
dyadic constants; and an r.e. set of logical axioms. Those fields make the
*deductive* apparatus of §7 effective, but they do not give computable access
to the rest of the syntax of `For(𝓛)`. In particular they do not let us compute,
from the code of \(\varphi\), the free variables of \(\varphi\), or the code of
its sup-closure (the sentence obtained by binding those variables).

Definition 9.10 and Corollary 9.11 ask for a *uniform* algorithm that, given
the code of a sentence, returns a dyadic approximation of \(\varphi^{\circ}_T\).
A computable function \(\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{Z}\) must halt on
*every* input code, including codes of open formulae and non-codes. Non-codes
are easy: the set of codes is decidable, so they can be rejected. Open formulae
are not. Over a complete theory, two-sided dyadic cuts close in on the value of
a *sentence*, because every model of \(T\) realises the same value. An open
formula may realise a positive gap between its infimum and its supremum across
models of \(T\) (for a metric, already \(d(x,c)\) depends on the assignment).
A naïve search for cuts of \(\varphi\) itself therefore need not halt on an
open-formula code.

One might hope to first test whether a code names a sentence and, if not, return
a dummy. That test is "the free-variable set is empty", which is not computable
from the earlier fields. Alternatively one can replace \(\varphi\) by its
sup-closure sentence, whose degree of truth, over a complete theory, can be
approximated by the same two-sided search; that replacement is exactly
`supClosureCode` below.

# A pathological numbering

The earlier operations present only a fragment of the formula algebra: the
set of codes, \(\dot{-}\), and the dyadic constants. A numbering may compute
those and still fail to compute free variables or sup-closure. Concretely, start
from a standard Gödel numbering \(e\), and use an undecidable set (for example
the halting set) to relocate the codes of formulae that lie outside the
subalgebra generated by dyadic constants under \(\dot{-}\) — in particular the
codes of quantified formulae and of atomic formulae with free variables —
while adjusting the table for \(\dot{-}\) so that it remains computable on the
relocated codes, and keeping the set of all codes decidable (for instance
recursive of finite complement, or all of \(\mathbb{N}\)). The logical axioms
remain r.e., because they are r.e. in \(e\) and the relocation of a recursively
enumerable set of axiom codes along an injective map with r.e. graph can be
arranged to stay r.e. Under such a numbering there is no algorithm that, given
\(\ulcorner\varphi\urcorner\), returns \(\ulcorner\sup^*\varphi\urcorner\) or
the list of free variables. Then no uniform procedure built from the old fields
can either recognise sentences or form their closures, and the two-sided search
fails to be total. So Corollary 9.11, as formalized, was not provable from those
fields: the Lean hypothesis was strictly weaker than the paper's intended
"countable, recursive language."

# Why `supClosureCode` matches the paper

A standard Gödel numbering of a recursive language makes *every* syntactic
operation computable: constructors and destructors for every clause of the
inductive definition of formulae, including \(\sup_x\), and hence the finite
list of free variables and the sup-closure. Adding a single computable map
`supClosureCode`, specified to send the code of \(\varphi\) to the code of its
sup-closure, is a minimal fragment of that standard package — exactly what the
totality argument for 9.11 uses, and nothing stronger. It does not add a new
mathematical hypothesis to the paper; it records, in the Lean model of
"countable, recursive language", a syntactic operation that any such
presentation already provides. See also `docs/recursive-presentation-cor-9.11.md`.
-/

/-- A *countable, recursive* presentation of a metric signature `𝓛` (the standing
assumption of the paper's decidability discussion, §1 and Corollary 9.11). -/
structure RecursivePresentation (LM : MetricSignature) where
  code : Formula LM.toSignature → ℕ
  code_injective : Function.Injective code
  isCode_computable : ComputablePred (fun n : ℕ => n ∈ Set.range code)
  tsubCode : ℕ → ℕ → ℕ
  tsubCode_computable : Computable₂ tsubCode
  tsubCode_spec : ∀ φ ψ, tsubCode (code φ) (code ψ) = code (.tsub φ ψ)
  dyadicCode : ℕ → ℕ → ℕ
  dyadicCode_computable : Computable₂ dyadicCode
  dyadicCode_spec : ∀ k n,
    dyadicCode k n = code (Formula.dyadic LM.toSignature (DyadicCode.mulPow2Neg k n))
  axioms_re : REPred (fun n : ℕ => ∃ φ ∈ metricAxiomSet LM, code φ = n)
  /-- The code of the sup-closure of a formula, computable from the formula's
      code. This is a standard syntactic operation of a Gödel numbering of a
      recursive language (§1), and is the minimal such operation used in the
      proof of Corollary 9.11. -/
  supClosureCode : ℕ → ℕ
  supClosureCode_computable : Computable supClosureCode
  supClosureCode_spec : ∀ φ, supClosureCode (code φ) = code φ.supClosure

end ContinuousFOL
